/* Rigorous Courses · Diffusion Models — Part 07: Running noise backwards: the true denoising step.
   Owns the exact reverse posterior q(x_{t-1} | x_t, x_0): why the unconditioned reverse step is
   unknowable, the full Bayes + complete-the-square derivation of mu-tilde and beta-tilde, the
   weighted-average reading, the small-beta Gaussian-shape argument, and the oracle sampler. */
(function () {
  window.LESSONS.push({
    id: "diff-07-reverse-posterior",
    title: "Part 7 — Running noise backwards: the true denoising step",
    tagline: "Bayes' rule plus complete-the-square turns the noising recipe around — the exact one-step denoiser is a Gaussian you can write down",
    module: "Rigorous Courses · Diffusion Models",
    superGroup: "Rigorous Courses",
    template: "course",
    part: 7,
    partTotal: 12,
    prereqs: ["diff-06-forward-process"],
    goal: `<p>After this part you can:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Explain, in plain English, why the reverse step $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$ is exactly what generation needs and exactly what we cannot compute directly &mdash; and why also conditioning on $x_0$ fixes that.</li>
        <li>Derive the true posterior $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$ from Bayes' rule and complete-the-square, justifying every single step.</li>
        <li>Compute the posterior mean $\\tilde\\mu_t$ and variance $\\tilde\\beta_t$ by hand for a concrete schedule, to four decimals.</li>
        <li>Read $\\tilde\\mu_t$ as a weighted average of the clean point and the noisy point, and predict how the two weights shift as $t$ changes.</li>
        <li>Run and explain the oracle sampler: a complete, working reverse-time generator that lacks only one input &mdash; a guess of $x_0$.</li>
      </ul>`,
    sections: [
      { h: "Where we are",
        body: `<p>Quick recap of the road so far. Part 1: generating means <b>sampling a new point from the data cloud's rule</b> &mdash; a rule we are never handed. Part 2 gave us the language of probability, including the tool of this chapter: <b>Bayes' rule</b>, which flips the direction of knowledge. Part 3 gave us the Gaussian toolkit &mdash; in particular the <b>complete-the-square</b> recipe and the fact that a product of Gaussian curves in the same variable is another Gaussian. Part 4 showed that a Markov chain can be run backwards if you do a Bayes flip <b>at every step</b>. Part 5 built the scoring tools (likelihood, KL, ELBO) that we will need again in part 8. And part 6 pinned down the <b>forward process</b>: the fixed noising recipe $q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})$, the closed form $q(x_t \\mid x_0)$, and the survival numbers $\\alpha_t$ and $\\bar\\alpha_t$ that track how much signal is left.</p>
        <p>So we can destroy data on schedule, and we know precisely what the wreckage looks like at every step. This part answers the question the whole course has been building toward: <b>what exactly is the perfect single denoising step?</b> That is, if you are standing at a noisy point $x_t$, what is the honest probability distribution over where the chain was one step earlier, at $x_{t-1}$? We will find that the direct version of this question is unanswerable &mdash; and that a slightly different version has an exact, beautiful, closed-form answer. This derivation is the mathematical heart of the course. Take it slowly; everything after it is applications.</p>` },

      { h: "The step we wish we could take",
        body: `<p>Remember the generation plan from part 1: start from pure noise $x_T$ and undo the noising one step at a time, walking $t = T, T-1, \\dots, 1$. Each undo needs the same object: given the current noisy point $x_t$, a rule for where the chain probably was one step earlier. In symbols, generation needs $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$ &mdash; read it as "the spread of possible previous points, given the current one".</p>
        <p>We know the chain in the <b>forward</b> direction: part 6 fixed $q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})$ by design. Flipping a conditional is exactly what <a onclick="App.open('prob-bayes')">Bayes' rule</a> from part 2 does, so let's try it:</p>
        <div class="formula-box">$$ q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t) \\;=\\; \\frac{q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})\\; q(x_{t-1})}{q(x_t)} $$</div>
        <p><b>Read it out loud:</b> the chance that the previous point was $x_{t-1}$, given that we now see $x_t$, equals the chance of stepping from $x_{t-1}$ to $x_t$ (the known forward recipe), times how likely the chain was to be at $x_{t-1}$ at all, divided by how likely it was to be at $x_t$ at all.</p>
        <p>The first factor is fine &mdash; it is the recipe we wrote ourselves. The trouble is the other two. The <b>marginal</b> $q(x_{t-1})$ asks: across all the data the chain might be fed, how often does step $t-1$ land at this value? To compute it you must average the closed form over every possible clean point: $q(x_{t-1}) = \\int q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_0)\\, q(x_0)\\, dx_0$ &mdash; in words, an area-style average (part 2) of known bell curves, <b>weighted by the data distribution $q(x_0)$</b>. And $q(x_0)$ is the data cloud's rule &mdash; the exact object this whole course is trying to learn. We have gone in a circle: to denoise perfectly, we would need to already know what realistic data looks like.</p>
        <p>Make it concrete. Suppose the data lives at just two values, $-2$ and $+2$, with equal odds (the notebook simulates exactly this). After heavy noising you observe $x_t = 0.1$. Where was the chain before? It could have drifted down from the $+2$ side or up from the $-2$ side &mdash; so the honest answer has <b>two bumps</b>, one for each possible origin, and how big each bump is depends on how often the data uses each side. Change the data, and the answer changes. So $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$ is not just unknown &mdash; it is not even Gaussian in general. The notebook shows you the two bumps in a histogram.</p>` },

      { h: "The fix: also condition on the clean point",
        body: `<p>Here is the move that unlocks everything. The reverse step is hard because we do not know where the chain <b>started</b>. So ask an easier question first: <b>what if we did know?</b> Condition on the clean point $x_0$ as well, and ask for $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$ &mdash; "given where the chain started AND where it is now, where was it one step ago?"</p>
        <p>An analogy. "Where was the hiker at 2pm?" is hard if all you know is their 3pm position &mdash; you would need to know which trailheads hikers tend to start from. But if you also know <b>this hiker's</b> trailhead, the question becomes pure geometry: somewhere along the way between start and current position, with a spread set by how erratically they walk. No knowledge of hikers-in-general needed. Conditioning on $x_0$ does the same for our chain: between a known start and a known present, everything in the middle is governed by the <b>fixed recipe alone</b>. The unknowable data distribution drops out of the problem.</p>
        <p>Now run Bayes' rule again &mdash; but inside the context "given $x_0$". Bayes' rule is derived from the definition of conditional probability (part 2), and that definition works the same when every probability in sight carries one extra condition along. So $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0) = q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1}, x_0)\\, q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_0) \\,/\\, q(x_t \\mid x_0)$.</p>
        <p>One simplification before we celebrate: look at the first factor, $q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1}, x_0)$. The step-$t$ recipe from part 6 is $x_t = \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\, x_{t-1} + \\sqrt{\\beta_t}\\, \\epsilon_t$: scale the previous point, add fresh noise. That recipe reads <b>only</b> $x_{t-1}$ &mdash; nowhere does $x_0$ appear in it. Once $x_{t-1}$ is fixed, the only randomness left in $x_t$ is the fresh noise $\\epsilon_t$, which is drawn independently of everything earlier. Being told $x_0$ on top changes nothing about how $x_t$ gets made. That is precisely the <b>Markov property</b> from part 4, and it lets us drop $x_0$ from the first factor: $q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1}, x_0) = q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})$. (Careful: this drop only works for the <b>downstream</b> variable given its immediate predecessor. It does NOT say $x_0$ is useless for guessing $x_{t-1}$ &mdash; it very much is not, as the final answer will show. Practice problem 4 pins this down.)</p>
        <div class="formula-box">$$ q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0) \\;=\\; \\frac{q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})\\; q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_0)}{q(x_t \\mid x_0)} $$</div>
        <p><b>Read it out loud:</b> the exact one-step denoiser &mdash; given both the current noisy point and the clean start &mdash; equals the forward recipe for the last step, times the closed-form jump from clean to step $t-1$, divided by the closed-form jump from clean to step $t$.</p>
        <p>Now look at what we are holding: <b>all three factors are Gaussians we derived in part 6.</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li>$q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1}) = \\mathcal{N}\\big(x_t;\\ \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\, x_{t-1},\\ \\beta_t\\big)$ &mdash; the fixed noising recipe (recall $\\alpha_t = 1-\\beta_t$, the fraction of variance kept at step $t$).</li>
          <li>$q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_0) = \\mathcal{N}\\big(x_{t-1};\\ \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0,\\ 1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}\\big)$ &mdash; the closed form at step $t-1$ (recall $\\bar\\alpha_t$, the fraction of the original signal surviving to step $t$).</li>
          <li>$q(x_t \\mid x_0) = \\mathcal{N}\\big(x_t;\\ \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t}\\, x_0,\\ 1-\\bar\\alpha_t\\big)$ &mdash; the closed form at step $t$.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Two housekeeping notes. First, we adopt the convention $\\bar\\alpha_0 = 1$: before any noising step, all of the signal survives (an empty product of survival factors is 1). This makes the formulas below work even at $t = 1$. Second, we will do the whole computation with plain numbers &mdash; the <b>scalar, per-coordinate</b> case. In many dimensions every Gaussian here has covariance proportional to the identity matrix $\\mathbf{I}$, which means each coordinate runs its own independent copy of this exact computation (part 3). Say "per coordinate" once, then work with scalars.</p>` },

      { h: "Setting up the algebra: three bells, one unknown",
        body: `<p>Before we compute, here is the strategy, so you never feel lost. We want a formula for a density <b>in the variable $x_{t-1}$</b> &mdash; that is the unknown we are asking about. The observed $x_t$ and the given $x_0$ are, for this computation, <b>known numbers</b> riding along. The plan, using part 3's tools: (i) write each Gaussian factor as an exponential of a quadratic penalty, (ii) multiply them by adding exponents, (iii) collect everything into one quadratic in $x_{t-1}$, (iv) complete the square, (v) pattern-match the result against the Gaussian's anatomy to read off the center and the spread.</p>
        <p>One workhorse idea makes this painless: the <b>constant bucket</b> $C$. Any factor that does not contain $x_{t-1}$ &mdash; normalizers, the whole denominator, leftover pieces of algebra &mdash; cannot change the <b>shape</b> of the curve over $x_{t-1}$; it only stretches the whole curve up or down. And a probability density has no freedom in its overall scale: the area under it must be 1 (part 3's normalizer logic). So we may sweep every such factor into one symbol $C$, do the shape work, and let "area = 1" set $C$ at the end. We never even need to compute $C$ explicitly.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Write Bayes' rule in the context of a known $x_0$,</b> giving $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0) = q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1}, x_0)\\, q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_0)\\,/\\,q(x_t \\mid x_0)$. <b>Why:</b> Bayes' rule follows from the definition of conditional probability (part 2), and that definition holds unchanged inside any fixed context &mdash; here, "given $x_0$" is carried by every factor.</li>
          <li><b>Drop $x_0$ from the first factor,</b> giving $q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})$ in its place. <b>Why:</b> the Markov property, argued in the previous section &mdash; the step-$t$ recipe reads only $x_{t-1}$, so extra knowledge of $x_0$ cannot alter the distribution of $x_t$.</li>
          <li><b>Replace the two numerator factors by their exponential forms:</b> the first contributes the exponent $-\\dfrac{(x_t - \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\, x_{t-1})^2}{2\\beta_t}$ and the second contributes $-\\dfrac{(x_{t-1} - \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0)^2}{2\\,(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}$. <b>Why:</b> part 3's anatomy of the Gaussian: $\\mathcal{N}(x; \\mu, \\sigma^2)$ is a normalizer times $e$ raised to minus the squared distance from the center, measured in units of the spread, halved. The centers and spreads are the part-6 facts listed above.</li>
          <li><b>Sweep the non-$x_{t-1}$ factors into the constant bucket:</b> both normalizers and the entire denominator $q(x_t \\mid x_0)$ go into $C$. <b>Why:</b> none of them contain $x_{t-1}$ &mdash; the denominator is a function of $x_t$ and $x_0$ only &mdash; so they cannot affect the shape in $x_{t-1}$, and "area = 1" will fix the scale at the end.</li>
          <li><b>Multiply the two surviving exponentials by adding their exponents.</b> <b>Why:</b> $e^{a} \\cdot e^{b} = e^{a+b}$ &mdash; the defining property of exponentials.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The result of the setup:</p>
        <div class="formula-box">$$ q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0) \\;=\\; C \\cdot \\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\left[\\frac{(x_t - \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\, x_{t-1})^2}{\\beta_t} \\;+\\; \\frac{(x_{t-1} - \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0)^2}{1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\right]\\right) $$</div>
        <p><b>Read it out loud:</b> the answer is some constant times $e$ to the minus one-half of two penalties added together: how far the observed $x_t$ sits from the scaled candidate $x_{t-1}$, in units of the fresh noise dose $\\beta_t$ &mdash; plus how far the candidate $x_{t-1}$ sits from the scaled clean point, in units of all the noise accumulated up to step $t-1$.</p>
        <p>Pause and appreciate the shape of the problem: the exponent is a <b>quadratic in $x_{t-1}$</b> (the unknown appears squared and to the first power, never worse). Part 3 told us exactly what to do with a quadratic exponent: complete the square, and a Gaussian will fall out. That is the next section.</p>` },

      { h: "The derivation: collect, complete the square, read off",
        body: `<p>Fifteen small steps, one operation each. We are massaging the bracketed expression $\\dfrac{(x_t - \\sqrt{\\alpha_t} x_{t-1})^2}{\\beta_t} + \\dfrac{(x_{t-1} - \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}} x_0)^2}{1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}$ from the box above, and the whole thing sits inside $\\exp(-\\tfrac12[\\cdots])$ throughout.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Expand the first square:</b> $(x_t - \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\, x_{t-1})^2 = x_t^2 - 2\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\, x_t\\, x_{t-1} + \\alpha_t\\, x_{t-1}^2$. <b>Why:</b> the identity $(a-b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2$, plus $(\\sqrt{\\alpha_t})^2 = \\alpha_t$.</li>
          <li><b>Expand the second square:</b> $(x_{t-1} - \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0)^2 = x_{t-1}^2 - 2\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0\\, x_{t-1} + \\bar\\alpha_{t-1}\\, x_0^2$. <b>Why:</b> the same identity, applied with $a = x_{t-1}$ and $b = \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0$.</li>
          <li><b>Sweep the $x_{t-1}$-free pieces into $C$:</b> the terms $x_t^2/\\beta_t$ and $\\bar\\alpha_{t-1} x_0^2/(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})$ leave the bracket. <b>Why:</b> neither contains $x_{t-1}$; inside $\\exp(-\\tfrac12[\\cdots])$ an added constant becomes a multiplied constant factor ($e^{a+b} = e^a e^b$ again), and multiplied constants live in $C$.</li>
          <li><b>Collect the $x_{t-1}^2$ terms:</b> their total coefficient is $A = \\dfrac{\\alpha_t}{\\beta_t} + \\dfrac{1}{1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}$. <b>Why:</b> grouping like powers of the unknown is how you see a quadratic's structure; one squared term came from each expanded square.</li>
          <li><b>Collect the $x_{t-1}$ terms:</b> both cross terms carry a factor $-2 x_{t-1}$, so the bracket now reads $A\\, x_{t-1}^2 - 2B\\, x_{t-1}$ with $B = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\, x_t}{\\beta_t} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0}{1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}$. <b>Why:</b> grouping like powers again; factoring the $-2$ out now sets us up to match part 3's complete-the-square recipe exactly.</li>
          <li><b>Put $A$ over one denominator:</b> $A = \\dfrac{\\alpha_t (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}) + \\beta_t}{\\beta_t (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}$. <b>Why:</b> adding fractions: $\\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{ad + cb}{bd}$. We simplify $A$ before completing the square because both the spread and the center will be built from it.</li>
          <li><b>Distribute $\\alpha_t$ in the numerator:</b> $\\alpha_t (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}) + \\beta_t = \\alpha_t - \\alpha_t\\, \\bar\\alpha_{t-1} + \\beta_t$. <b>Why:</b> multiplying out prepares the two identities that come next.</li>
          <li><b>Recognize the running product:</b> $\\alpha_t\\, \\bar\\alpha_{t-1} = \\bar\\alpha_t$, so the numerator becomes $\\alpha_t - \\bar\\alpha_t + \\beta_t$. <b>Why:</b> by definition $\\bar\\alpha_t = \\prod_{s=1}^{t} \\alpha_s$, so multiplying the product-up-to-$t-1$ by one more factor $\\alpha_t$ gives the product-up-to-$t$. This "peel one factor" identity returns in part 9 &mdash; learn to spot it.</li>
          <li><b>Use $\\alpha_t + \\beta_t = 1$:</b> the numerator becomes $1 - \\bar\\alpha_t$, so $A = \\dfrac{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}{\\beta_t (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}$. <b>Why:</b> $\\alpha_t = 1 - \\beta_t$ by definition &mdash; survival fraction plus noise dose is 1.</li>
          <li><b>Complete the square:</b> $A x_{t-1}^2 - 2 B x_{t-1} = A\\left(x_{t-1} - \\dfrac{B}{A}\\right)^2 - \\dfrac{B^2}{A}$. <b>Why:</b> part 3's four-step recipe (factor out $A$, add and subtract the square of half the linear coefficient, regroup). You can verify it in one line: expand the right side and the $x_{t-1}$ terms match.</li>
          <li><b>Sweep $B^2/A$ into $C$:</b> only the $A\\,(x_{t-1} - B/A)^2$ piece stays in the bracket. <b>Why:</b> $B$ and $A$ are built from $x_t$, $x_0$ and schedule numbers &mdash; no $x_{t-1}$ anywhere &mdash; so this term is another constant factor after exponentiating.</li>
          <li><b>Pattern-match against the Gaussian anatomy:</b> the density is now $C' \\exp\\!\\left(-\\dfrac{(x_{t-1} - B/A)^2}{2\\,(1/A)}\\right)$, which is exactly $\\mathcal{N}\\big(x_{t-1};\\ B/A,\\ 1/A\\big)$. <b>Why:</b> part 3: a density of the form $\\exp(-(x-m)^2 / (2 s^2))$, times whatever normalizer area demands, is the bell curve with center $m$ and variance $s^2$. This also confirms part 3's promise that a product of Gaussians in the same variable stays Gaussian.</li>
          <li><b>Read off the variance:</b> $\\tilde\\beta_t = \\dfrac{1}{A} = \\dfrac{\\beta_t\\, (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}$. <b>Why:</b> taking the reciprocal of a fraction swaps its numerator and denominator; we simplified $A$ in step 9 precisely so this flip would be clean.</li>
          <li><b>Read off the center as $\\tilde\\mu_t = B/A = B \\cdot \\tilde\\beta_t$.</b> <b>Why:</b> dividing by $A$ is the same operation as multiplying by $1/A$, and $1/A$ is the $\\tilde\\beta_t$ we just found.</li>
          <li><b>Multiply out the first term of $B \\cdot \\tilde\\beta_t$:</b> $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\, x_t}{\\beta_t} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\beta_t (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\,(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}\\, x_t$. <b>Why:</b> the factor $\\beta_t$ appears in a numerator and a denominator, so it cancels.</li>
          <li><b>Multiply out the second term:</b> $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0}{1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\beta_t (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, \\beta_t}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}\\, x_0$; adding the two terms gives $\\tilde\\mu_t$. <b>Why:</b> the factor $(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})$ cancels the same way, and $B$ was a sum of two terms, so $B \\cdot \\tilde\\beta_t$ distributes across both.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Collecting the results, here is the destination of the whole chapter:</p>
        <div class="formula-box">$$ q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0) \\;=\\; \\mathcal{N}\\big(x_{t-1};\\ \\tilde\\mu_t(x_t, x_0),\\ \\tilde\\beta_t\\big) $$</div>
        <p><b>Read it out loud:</b> given the current noisy point and the clean start, the previous point is exactly Gaussian &mdash; no approximation anywhere &mdash; with a center and a spread we can now state in closed form.</p>
        <div class="formula-box">$$ \\tilde\\beta_t \\;=\\; \\frac{(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})\\, \\beta_t}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t} $$</div>
        <p><b>Read it out loud:</b> the true denoiser's spread is the fresh noise dose $\\beta_t$, shrunk by the ratio of noise-accumulated-before ($1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}$) to noise-accumulated-after ($1-\\bar\\alpha_t$). Since the before-noise is always less than the after-noise, this spread is always smaller than $\\beta_t$: knowing the start shrinks your uncertainty about the step.</p>
        <div class="formula-box">$$ \\tilde\\mu_t(x_t, x_0) \\;=\\; \\frac{\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, \\beta_t\\; x_0 \\;+\\; \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\,(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})\\; x_t}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t} $$</div>
        <p><b>Read it out loud:</b> the best guess for the previous point blends two anchors &mdash; the clean point $x_0$, weighted by the surviving-signal factor $\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}$ times the fresh dose $\\beta_t$, and the current point $x_t$, weighted by the one-step survival factor $\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}$ times the noise accumulated before the step &mdash; all divided by the total accumulated noise $1-\\bar\\alpha_t$.</p>
        <p>These are equations 6 and 7 of the <a onclick="App.open('paper-ddpm')">DDPM paper</a>, derived with nothing but part 2's Bayes rule and part 3's algebra. Every diffusion model in production computes with these two formulas.</p>` },

      { h: "What the answer says: a tug-of-war between two anchors",
        body: `<p>Formulas earn their keep when you can read them. Give the two weights names:</p>
        <div class="formula-box">$$ \\tilde\\mu_t = a_t\\, x_0 + b_t\\, x_t, \\qquad a_t = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, \\beta_t}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}, \\qquad b_t = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\,(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t} $$</div>
        <p><b>Read it out loud:</b> the posterior center is a weighted blend of where you started ($x_0$, weight $a_t$) and where you are now ($x_t$, weight $b_t$).</p>
        <p>The two anchors pull against each other, and the schedule referees the tug-of-war. Here is the whole story on our running example from part 6 &mdash; $T = 3$, $\\beta = (0.1, 0.2, 0.3)$, so $\\alpha = (0.9, 0.8, 0.7)$ and $\\bar\\alpha = (0.9, 0.72, 0.504)$, with $\\bar\\alpha_0 = 1$:</p>
        <table class="symbols">
          <tr><td><b>step $t$</b></td><td><b>weight on $x_0$: $a_t$</b></td><td><b>weight on $x_t$: $b_t$</b></td><td><b>spread $\\tilde\\beta_t$</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>1.0000</td><td>0.0000</td><td>0.0000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>0.6776</td><td>0.3194</td><td>0.0714</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>0.5132</td><td>0.4723</td><td>0.1694</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p>Read the trend down the columns: as $t$ grows, the weight slides from the $x_0$ anchor toward the $x_t$ anchor, and the spread $\\tilde\\beta_t$ grows. At heavy noise the current position is (relatively) the more informative anchor about the immediate past; near the start, the clean point dominates. With the real production schedule ($T = 1000$, tiny doses), the pattern is far more extreme: $b_t$ hugs 1 for almost the whole chain, and only the last few steps swing hard toward $x_0$ &mdash; the notebook plots both weight curves for $T = 200$ so you can watch the handover.</p>
        <p>Two limiting cases to check the reading (these are exactly the sanity checks a professional would run):</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Tiny dose, $\\beta_t \\to 0$:</b> then $\\alpha_t \\to 1$ and $\\bar\\alpha_t \\to \\bar\\alpha_{t-1}$, so $a_t \\to 0$, $b_t \\to 1$, and $\\tilde\\beta_t \\to 0$. The posterior says: stay exactly where you are. Correct &mdash; a step that added no noise is undone by not moving.</li>
          <li><b>First step, $t = 1$ (using $\\bar\\alpha_0 = 1$):</b> then $1 - \\bar\\alpha_0 = 0$, so $b_1 = 0$, $\\tilde\\beta_1 = 0$, and $a_1 = \\beta_1 / (1-\\bar\\alpha_1) = \\beta_1/\\beta_1 = 1$. The posterior collapses to a zero-spread spike at exactly $x_0$. Correct &mdash; if someone hands you $x_0$, the question "what was the value before the first dose?" answers itself, and the observed $x_1$ is rightly ignored.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>One small honest note: $a_t + b_t$ is close to 1 but not exactly 1 (at $t = 2$: $0.6776 + 0.3194 = 0.9971$). That is fine. This is not a plain average of two positions &mdash; the anchors enter <b>rescaled</b> by survival factors, so their weights have no obligation to sum to 1.</p>` },

      { h: "A posterior you can compute on paper",
        body: `<p>Time for real numbers, start to finish. Running example, step $t = 2$: the clean point is $x_0 = 2$, and we observed $x_2 = 1.1$. Find $\\tilde\\mu_2$ and $\\tilde\\beta_2$ to four decimals. Carry six decimals in the intermediate lines and round once at the end.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Gather the schedule numbers:</b> $\\beta_2 = 0.2$, $\\alpha_2 = 0.8$, $\\bar\\alpha_1 = 0.9$, $\\bar\\alpha_2 = 0.72$. <b>Why:</b> these came from part 6's running example; everything below is arithmetic on them.</li>
          <li><b>Compute the two accumulated-noise terms:</b> $1 - \\bar\\alpha_1 = 0.1$ and $1 - \\bar\\alpha_2 = 0.28$. <b>Why:</b> both appear in all three formulas; computing them once avoids slips.</li>
          <li><b>Spread:</b> $\\tilde\\beta_2 = \\dfrac{0.1 \\times 0.2}{0.28} = \\dfrac{0.02}{0.28} = 0.071429$. <b>Why:</b> plug into $\\tilde\\beta_t = (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$.</li>
          <li><b>Weight on $x_0$:</b> $\\sqrt{0.9} = 0.948683$, so $a_2 = \\dfrac{0.948683 \\times 0.2}{0.28} = \\dfrac{0.189737}{0.28} = 0.677631$. <b>Why:</b> plug into $a_t = \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\,\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$.</li>
          <li><b>Weight on $x_t$:</b> $\\sqrt{0.8} = 0.894427$, so $b_2 = \\dfrac{0.894427 \\times 0.1}{0.28} = \\dfrac{0.089443}{0.28} = 0.319438$. <b>Why:</b> plug into $b_t = \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\,(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$.</li>
          <li><b>Blend the anchors:</b> $\\tilde\\mu_2 = 0.677631 \\times 2 + 0.319438 \\times 1.1 = 1.355262 + 0.351382 = 1.706644$. <b>Why:</b> $\\tilde\\mu_t = a_t x_0 + b_t x_t$ with the weights from steps 4-5.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Final answers: $\\tilde\\mu_2 = 1.7066$ and $\\tilde\\beta_2 = 0.0714$. Now read the number, don't just compute it. The observed $x_2 = 1.1$ is unusually low: a clean $x_0 = 2$ sits, on average, at $\\sqrt{0.72} \\times 2 = 1.697$ by step 2, and undoing just the last step's scaling of $x_2$ alone would suggest $1.1 / \\sqrt{0.8} = 1.230$. Meanwhile $x_0$ alone predicts $x_1$ near $\\sqrt{0.9} \\times 2 = 1.897$. The posterior mean $1.7066$ lands between those two stories, closer to the $x_0$ side &mdash; the formula judged the low $x_2$ to be partly a noise fluke and split the difference by reliability. The notebook checks these exact four decimals, and then verifies the whole distribution &mdash; center AND spread &mdash; against 200,000 brute-force simulated chains.</p>` },

      { h: "Why a bell curve is the right shape for small steps",
        body: `<p>A worry may be nagging you. In part 8 we will build a learned denoiser $p_\\theta(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$ &mdash; note: <b>without</b> being told $x_0$ &mdash; and we will model it as a Gaussian. But the first section of this part showed that the true $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$ can be <b>two-bumped</b>, not Gaussian at all. Are we building our model on a falsehood?</p>
        <p>No &mdash; and the escape hatch is the size of the step. The two-bump demo used our teaching schedule: three huge doses ($\\beta$ up to 0.3). Real diffusion models take a thousand tiny steps, and for tiny steps the flip of a Gaussian step is nearly Gaussian. Here is the intuition, in two pictures.</p>
        <p><b>The zoom argument.</b> A smooth curve looks like a straight line if you zoom in far enough &mdash; that is the whole idea of a slope. Likewise, the data cloud's density is a smooth landscape, and a single small noise step can only move a point a tiny distance. Over that tiny neighborhood the landscape looks flat (or gently tilted). Bayes-flipping a Gaussian step against a flat prior gives back a Gaussian; against a gently tilted one, a Gaussian with a slightly shifted center. The bumps you saw came from the landscape varying <b>across</b> the step &mdash; which only happens when the step is big.</p>
        <p><b>The merging-bumps argument, slightly sharper.</b> Write the true reverse step as a blend over possible origins: $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$ is an average of our exact Gaussian posteriors $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$, one per candidate $x_0$, weighted by how plausible each origin is. Each component has spread $\\tilde\\beta_t$, and their centers differ only through the term $a_t\\, x_0$. Now shrink $\\beta_t$: the centers' disagreement $a_t$ shrinks in proportion to $\\beta_t$, while each bell's width $\\sqrt{\\tilde\\beta_t}$ shrinks only like $\\sqrt{\\beta_t}$ &mdash; much more slowly. So as the doses get small, the component bells crowd together <b>faster than they narrow</b>, and the bumps merge into one approximately Gaussian bell. For big doses the argument runs in reverse, and you get the two bumps of the notebook.</p>
        <p>Honesty box: making "nearly Gaussian" precise takes real analysis &mdash; the classical statement is in Feller's 1949 work on reversing diffusion processes, and Sohl-Dickstein and coauthors (2015) built the first diffusion generative model on exactly this foundation. We take the result on trust at full rigor, but the notebook's histograms let you watch it become true as steps shrink &mdash; and none of this affects today's main theorem: $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$ is <b>exactly</b> Gaussian, no approximation, for any step size. The approximation question concerns only the $x_0$-free version that the network will have to imitate. And it explains a design choice you might have wondered about since part 6: <b>this is why $T$ is large and each $\\beta_t$ is small.</b></p>` },

      { h: "The oracle sampler, and the honest gap",
        body: `<p>Let's cash in the theorem. Suppose &mdash; purely as a thought experiment &mdash; that whenever you needed it, an <b>oracle</b> whispered a valid clean point $x_0$ in your ear. Could you generate data? Yes, perfectly, with nothing but this part's formulas:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Draw $x_T$ from $\\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})$</b> &mdash; pure noise. <b>Why:</b> part 6 showed the forward chain ends, for all practical purposes, at exactly this distribution, so it is the correct starting pool.</li>
          <li><b>For $t = T, T-1, \\dots, 1$: draw $x_{t-1}$ from $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$</b> using the oracle's $x_0$ &mdash; a Gaussian draw with center $a_t x_0 + b_t x_t$ and spread $\\tilde\\beta_t$, which by part 3's reparameterization is one line: $x_{t-1} = \\tilde\\mu_t + \\sqrt{\\tilde\\beta_t}\\, z$ with fresh $z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$. <b>Why:</b> each draw is the exact reverse conditional we derived &mdash; no approximation enters at any step.</li>
          <li><b>Stop at $t = 1$; output $x_0$'s reconstruction.</b> The last step needs no special handling: the formulas themselves set $\\tilde\\beta_1 = 0$ and $\\tilde\\mu_1 = x_0$, so the walk lands exactly on the oracle's point. <b>Why:</b> the $t = 1$ limit computed in the weight table.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The notebook builds this in two dimensions: 2,000 pure-noise points, an 8-cluster ring dataset, and the true-posterior walk with each point's oracle $x_0$. Across six snapshots you watch formless noise <b>reassemble into the ring</b>. It is worth sitting with that picture: every arrow of time in it is one of today's Gaussian draws. Reverse-time generation is not a metaphor &mdash; it is a sequence of formulas you have now derived and can verify.</p>
        <p>And now the honest gap. The oracle sampler is a proof of concept, not a generator: it needs $x_0$ as an <b>input</b>, and $x_0$ &mdash; a brand-new clean data point &mdash; is precisely the thing generation is supposed to <b>produce</b>. Nobody whispers answers at sampling time. But look at how narrow the gap is: the entire reverse-time machine works, and the only missing part is <b>a guess of $x_0$ given $x_t$</b> to slot into $\\tilde\\mu_t$'s blend. That is a prediction problem &mdash; and prediction is what neural networks are for. Part 8 turns "imitate the true denoiser at every step" into a single trainable number using part 5's ELBO. Part 9 then reveals the industry's favorite form of the guess: train a network $\\epsilon_\\theta(x_t, t)$ to predict the <b>noise</b> $\\epsilon$ inside $x_t$ &mdash; which, by inverting part 6's one-line sampler, is the same information as $x_0$ &mdash; and plug that guess into today's formula. The formulas you just derived survive, byte for byte, inside Stable Diffusion.</p>` },

      { h: "Sanity checks",
        body: `<p>Run the main results through every cheap test we have. Each check catches a real class of derivation error, and all of them pass.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>The spread is positive.</b> $\\tilde\\beta_t$ is a product and ratio of strictly positive quantities ($\\beta_t &gt; 0$, and $\\bar\\alpha$ values sit strictly between 0 and 1 for $t \\ge 1$), so $\\tilde\\beta_t &gt; 0$ for $t \\ge 2$, and $= 0$ exactly at $t = 1$. A negative variance would have meant an algebra sign error.</li>
          <li><b>Knowing more never widens the spread, part 1:</b> $\\tilde\\beta_t \\le \\beta_t$, because the ratio $(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$ is below 1 ($\\bar\\alpha$ shrinks as $t$ grows, so the numerator's accumulated noise is the smaller one). Numbers: $\\tilde\\beta_2 = 0.0714 \\le 0.2 = \\beta_2$; $\\tilde\\beta_3 = 0.1694 \\le 0.3 = \\beta_3$.</li>
          <li><b>Knowing more never widens the spread, part 2:</b> $\\tilde\\beta_t \\le 1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}$, the spread of $x_{t-1}$ given only $x_0$ &mdash; because $\\tilde\\beta_t$ equals that spread times $\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$, and the derivation's step 9 showed $1-\\bar\\alpha_t = \\beta_t + \\alpha_t(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}) \\ge \\beta_t$, making the multiplier at most 1. Numbers: $0.0714 \\le 0.1$. Seeing $x_t$ on top of $x_0$ sharpened the answer, as it must.</li>
          <li><b>The do-nothing limit:</b> $\\beta_t \\to 0$ gives $\\tilde\\mu_t \\to x_t$, $\\tilde\\beta_t \\to 0$: a step that added no noise is undone by staying put (worked in full in practice problem 3).</li>
          <li><b>The first-step limit:</b> at $t = 1$, the posterior is a spike at exactly $x_0$ &mdash; the formula detects that conditioning on $x_0$ answers the $t=1$ question outright.</li>
          <li><b>The consistency (averages) check.</b> Feed the formula the <b>average</b> value of $x_t$ given $x_0$, namely $\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t}\\, x_0$, and it should return the average of $x_{t-1}$ given $x_0$, namely $\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0$. It does: $a_t x_0 + b_t \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t} x_0$ has numerator $\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\big[\\beta_t + \\alpha_t (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})\\big] x_0$ (using $\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t} = \\alpha_t \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}$, the peel-one-factor identity under a square root), and the bracket is $1-\\bar\\alpha_t$ by derivation step 9 &mdash; so everything cancels to $\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\, x_0$ exactly. Numbers at $t=2$, $x_0 = 2$: plug $x_2 = 1.697056$ and get $0.677631 \\times 2 + 0.319438 \\times 1.697056 = 1.897366 = \\sqrt{0.9} \\times 2$. The two anchors' odd-looking weights conspire to keep averages exactly consistent &mdash; strong evidence no algebra slipped.</li>
          <li><b>Shape check.</b> The result is Gaussian in $x_{t-1}$ &mdash; exactly what part 3 promised for a product of Gaussians in one variable. The denominator of Bayes never threatened this: it carried no $x_{t-1}$ at all.</li>
        </ul>` },

      { h: "What you can do now",
        body: `<p>You can now do the single most important computation in diffusion models. Concretely, you can: explain why $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$ needs the unknowable data distribution while $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$ needs only the fixed recipe; run the Bayes-plus-complete-the-square derivation and justify all fifteen algebra steps; state $\\tilde\\mu_t$ and $\\tilde\\beta_t$ from memory and compute them by hand ($\\tilde\\mu_2 = 1.7066$, $\\tilde\\beta_2 = 0.0714$ on the running example); read the posterior mean as a schedule-refereed tug-of-war between the $x_0$ anchor and the $x_t$ anchor; say why small steps make the Gaussian shape legitimate for the learned reverse model; and run the oracle sampler, which generates perfectly given one input we do not have.</p>
        <p>That missing input frames everything that remains. We need a network to stand in for the oracle &mdash; which raises the question part 8 answers: <b>how do you score such a network with one number you can train on?</b> The answer reuses part 5's ELBO on the diffusion chain. It will decompose, beautifully, into a sum of per-step comparisons &mdash; each one a KL divergence between today's true posterior $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$ and the learned denoiser $p_\\theta(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$. And because both of those are Gaussians, part 5's closed-form KL turns every term into concrete algebra. Today's formula is about to become the training target.</p>` }
    ],
    symbols: [
      { sym: "$x_0$", desc: "the clean data point (image as a list of numbers); in this part, also the extra thing we condition on" },
      { sym: "$x_t$, $x_{t-1}$", desc: "the data after $t$ (respectively $t-1$) small doses of noise; $x_{t-1}$ is the unknown the posterior asks about" },
      { sym: "$T$", desc: "total number of noising steps (3 in the hand example, 200 in the notebook's schedule)" },
      { sym: "$\\beta_t$", desc: "how much fresh noise step $t$ adds (a small number like 0.0001-0.02; 0.1-0.3 in the teaching example)" },
      { sym: "$\\alpha_t = 1 - \\beta_t$", desc: "the fraction of variance kept at step $t$" },
      { sym: "$\\bar\\alpha_t = \\prod_{s=1}^{t} \\alpha_s$", desc: "the fraction of the original signal surviving to step $t$; convention $\\bar\\alpha_0 = 1$ (no steps, nothing destroyed)" },
      { sym: "$q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})$", desc: "the fixed noising recipe: $\\mathcal{N}(\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\,x_{t-1},\\ \\beta_t)$ per coordinate" },
      { sym: "$q(x_t \\mid x_0)$", desc: "the closed-form jump from clean to step $t$: $\\mathcal{N}(\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t}\\,x_0,\\ 1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$ per coordinate" },
      { sym: "$q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$", desc: "the true posterior: the exact one-step denoiser IF you knew the clean point $x_0$" },
      { sym: "$\\tilde\\mu_t(x_t, x_0)$", desc: "the posterior's center: the best guess for $x_{t-1}$, a blend $a_t x_0 + b_t x_t$" },
      { sym: "$\\tilde\\beta_t$", desc: "the posterior's spread: $(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})\\,\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$, always at most $\\beta_t$" },
      { sym: "$a_t$, $b_t$", desc: "the blend weights on $x_0$ and $x_t$ inside $\\tilde\\mu_t$; $a_t = \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$, $b_t = \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$" },
      { sym: "$\\mathcal{N}(x;\\, \\mu, \\sigma^2)$", desc: "bell curve over $x$ with center $\\mu$ and spread $\\sigma^2$" },
      { sym: "$C$", desc: "the constant bucket: every factor with no $x_{t-1}$ in it; fixed at the end by the rule that a density's area is 1" },
      { sym: "$A$, $B$", desc: "the collected coefficients of $x_{t-1}^2$ and $x_{t-1}$ in the exponent; center $= B/A$, variance $= 1/A$" },
      { sym: "$p_\\theta(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$", desc: "the learned one-step denoiser (no $x_0$ input!) that parts 8-10 will train to imitate the true posterior" },
      { sym: "$\\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})$", desc: "fresh standard Gaussian noise; part 9 shows guessing $\\epsilon$ is the same as guessing $x_0$" }
    ],
    recall: [
      "Generation needs the reverse conditional $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$. Which single unknown object blocks us from computing it with Bayes' rule?",
      "What extra variable do we condition on to make the reverse step exactly computable, and why does that variable remove the need to know the data distribution?",
      "State the Markov drop used in the derivation ($q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1}, x_0) = q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})$) and give the one-sentence reason it is allowed.",
      "Write $\\tilde\\beta_t$ from memory. Why is it always smaller than $\\beta_t$?",
      "In the blend $\\tilde\\mu_t = a_t x_0 + b_t x_t$: which anchor gets more weight at heavy noise (large $t$), and which wins at $t = 1$?",
      "Why is a Gaussian a legitimate shape for the learned reverse step when every $\\beta_t$ is small, even though $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$ can be two-bumped for big steps?",
      "The oracle sampler generates perfectly. What is the one input it consumes that a real generator cannot have?"
    ],
    practice: [
      { q: "The quadratic-coefficient shortcut. In the derivation, once the exponent reads $-\\tfrac12(A x_{t-1}^2 - 2B x_{t-1})$ plus constants, the variance of the resulting Gaussian is $1/A$ &mdash; no completing the square needed for the spread alone. Use this shortcut: start from $A = \\dfrac{\\alpha_t}{\\beta_t} + \\dfrac{1}{1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}$ and re-derive $\\tilde\\beta_t$.",
        steps: [
          { do: "Put $A$ over one denominator: $A = \\dfrac{\\alpha_t(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}) + \\beta_t}{\\beta_t(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}$.", why: "Adding fractions, $\\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{ad+cb}{bd}$; one denominator makes the upcoming flip a single move." },
          { do: "Distribute $\\alpha_t$ in the numerator, giving $\\alpha_t - \\alpha_t\\bar\\alpha_{t-1} + \\beta_t$.", why: "Multiplying out exposes the product $\\alpha_t\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}$, which the next identity simplifies." },
          { do: "Replace $\\alpha_t\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}$ by $\\bar\\alpha_t$, giving numerator $\\alpha_t - \\bar\\alpha_t + \\beta_t$.", why: "The running product $\\bar\\alpha_t = \\prod_{s=1}^{t}\\alpha_s$ is the product up to $t-1$ times one more factor $\\alpha_t$ &mdash; the peel-one-factor identity." },
          { do: "Replace $\\alpha_t + \\beta_t$ by $1$, giving numerator $1 - \\bar\\alpha_t$, so $A = \\dfrac{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}{\\beta_t(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}$.", why: "By definition $\\alpha_t = 1-\\beta_t$: survival fraction plus noise dose is 1." },
          { do: "Flip the fraction: $\\tilde\\beta_t = 1/A = \\dfrac{\\beta_t(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})}{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}$.", why: "The reciprocal of a fraction swaps numerator and denominator, and the shortcut says the variance is exactly $1/A$." }
        ],
        answer: "$\\tilde\\beta_t = (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})\\,\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$ &mdash; the same spread the full derivation produced, reached with less work because the variance never depends on the linear coefficient $B$." },

      { q: "A fresh weight table. Take a new two-step schedule: $T = 2$ with $\\beta = (0.2, 0.5)$. Build $\\alpha$ and $\\bar\\alpha$, then compute the $t = 2$ posterior ingredients $a_2$ (weight on $x_0$), $b_2$ (weight on $x_t$), and $\\tilde\\beta_2$, each to four decimals.",
        steps: [
          { do: "Compute the survival factors: $\\alpha = (0.8, 0.5)$.", why: "$\\alpha_t = 1 - \\beta_t$, applied to each dose." },
          { do: "Compute the running products: $\\bar\\alpha_1 = 0.8$ and $\\bar\\alpha_2 = 0.8 \\times 0.5 = 0.4$.", why: "$\\bar\\alpha_t$ multiplies all survival factors up to step $t$." },
          { do: "Compute the accumulated-noise terms: $1-\\bar\\alpha_1 = 0.2$ and $1-\\bar\\alpha_2 = 0.6$.", why: "Both appear in every posterior formula; do them once." },
          { do: "Weight on $x_0$: $a_2 = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{0.8} \\times 0.5}{0.6} = \\dfrac{0.894427 \\times 0.5}{0.6} = \\dfrac{0.447214}{0.6} = 0.745356 \\approx 0.7454$.", why: "Plug into $a_t = \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\,\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$." },
          { do: "Weight on $x_t$: $b_2 = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{0.5} \\times 0.2}{0.6} = \\dfrac{0.707107 \\times 0.2}{0.6} = \\dfrac{0.141421}{0.6} = 0.235702 \\approx 0.2357$.", why: "Plug into $b_t = \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\,(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$." },
          { do: "Spread: $\\tilde\\beta_2 = \\dfrac{0.2 \\times 0.5}{0.6} = \\dfrac{0.1}{0.6} = 0.166667 \\approx 0.1667$.", why: "Plug into $\\tilde\\beta_t = (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})\\,\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$." }
        ],
        answer: "$a_2 \\approx 0.7454$, $b_2 \\approx 0.2357$, $\\tilde\\beta_2 \\approx 0.1667$. Note the story the numbers tell: step 2's dose was enormous ($\\beta_2 = 0.5$), so the noisy point is unreliable and the clean anchor $x_0$ carries most of the weight." },

      { q: "The do-nothing limit. Show that as $\\beta_t \\to 0$ (with $t &gt; 1$ fixed, so $\\bar\\alpha_{t-1} &lt; 1$): $a_t \\to 0$, $b_t \\to 1$, and $\\tilde\\beta_t \\to 0$. Then say in one sentence why this is the right answer.",
        steps: [
          { do: "Note $\\beta_t \\to 0$ forces $\\alpha_t \\to 1$.", why: "$\\alpha_t = 1 - \\beta_t$: no dose means full survival." },
          { do: "Deduce $\\bar\\alpha_t \\to \\bar\\alpha_{t-1}$, so $1-\\bar\\alpha_t \\to 1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}$, a positive number.", why: "$\\bar\\alpha_t = \\alpha_t\\,\\bar\\alpha_{t-1} \\to 1 \\cdot \\bar\\alpha_{t-1}$; positivity holds because $t &gt; 1$ means some noise already accumulated." },
          { do: "Take the limit of $a_t = \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\,\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$: the numerator carries the factor $\\beta_t \\to 0$ while the denominator tends to $1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1} &gt; 0$, so $a_t \\to 0$.", why: "A fraction whose top goes to zero and whose bottom stays away from zero goes to zero." },
          { do: "Take the limit of $b_t = \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\,(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t) \\to 1 \\cdot (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})/(1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}) = 1$.", why: "Substituting the limits from the first two steps; the matching numerator and denominator cancel." },
          { do: "Take the limit of $\\tilde\\beta_t = (1-\\bar\\alpha_{t-1})\\,\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t) \\to 0$.", why: "Again the factor $\\beta_t \\to 0$ in the numerator wins against a denominator bounded away from zero." }
        ],
        answer: "In the limit the posterior says $x_{t-1} = x_t$ exactly, with zero spread: a step that added no noise is undone by not moving. The formula passes the do-nothing test." },

      { q: "The Markov drop, and its trap. Justify carefully why $q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1}, x_0) = q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})$, and then explain why the superficially similar claim \"$x_0$ can be dropped from $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$\" is FALSE.",
        steps: [
          { do: "Write the step-$t$ mechanism as an update rule: $x_t = \\sqrt{\\alpha_t}\\, x_{t-1} + \\sqrt{\\beta_t}\\, \\epsilon_t$ with fresh noise $\\epsilon_t \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$.", why: "This is part 6's forward kernel written as the recipe it implements; a distributional claim is easiest to check on the mechanism." },
          { do: "Observe that the rule consumes exactly two inputs &mdash; $x_{t-1}$ and the fresh $\\epsilon_t$ &mdash; and that $x_0$ appears nowhere in it.", why: "Once $x_{t-1}$ is fixed, all remaining randomness in $x_t$ comes from $\\epsilon_t$, which is drawn independently of the past." },
          { do: "Conclude the distribution of $x_t$ given $x_{t-1}$ is identical whether or not $x_0$ is also revealed: $q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1}, x_0) = q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})$ &mdash; the Markov property of part 4.", why: "Extra information that the generating mechanism never consults cannot change the outcome's distribution." },
          { do: "For the trap: note that $x_{t-1}$ is produced from $x_0$ BEFORE $x_t$ exists, so $x_0$ genuinely constrains $x_{t-1}$ even after $x_t$ is seen &mdash; the $t=2$ running example gives $x_0$ the weight $a_2 = 0.6776$, not $0$.", why: "The Markov property screens the FUTURE off from the deeper past given the present; it never says the past is redundant for guessing the present. The two claims condition in opposite directions." }
        ],
        answer: "Dropping $x_0$ is legal only in $q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1}, x_0)$, where the conditioned-on $x_{t-1}$ is the sole input of the mechanism that makes $x_t$. In $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$ the roles reverse &mdash; and the derived weight $a_t &gt; 0$ is the proof that $x_0$ stays informative." },

      { q: "The posterior at $t = 1$, by hand. Using the running schedule ($\\beta_1 = 0.1$, $\\bar\\alpha_1 = 0.9$, convention $\\bar\\alpha_0 = 1$), the clean point $x_0 = 2$, and an observed $x_1 = 1.5$: compute $a_1$, $b_1$, $\\tilde\\beta_1$, and $\\tilde\\mu_1$.",
        steps: [
          { do: "Recall the convention $\\bar\\alpha_0 = 1$, so $1 - \\bar\\alpha_0 = 0$.", why: "Before any noising step the whole signal survives &mdash; an empty product of survival factors is 1." },
          { do: "Compute $1 - \\bar\\alpha_1 = 1 - 0.9 = 0.1$.", why: "This is the denominator of all three formulas at $t = 1$." },
          { do: "Weight on $x_0$: $a_1 = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{1} \\times 0.1}{0.1} = 1$.", why: "Plug $\\bar\\alpha_0 = 1$ and $\\beta_1 = 0.1$ into $a_t = \\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_{t-1}}\\,\\beta_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)$." },
          { do: "Weight on $x_1$: $b_1 = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{0.9} \\times 0}{0.1} = 0$.", why: "The factor $1-\\bar\\alpha_0 = 0$ kills the whole term regardless of the other factors." },
          { do: "Spread: $\\tilde\\beta_1 = \\dfrac{0 \\times 0.1}{0.1} = 0$.", why: "The same zero factor $1-\\bar\\alpha_0$ appears in the numerator of $\\tilde\\beta_t$." },
          { do: "Center: $\\tilde\\mu_1 = 1 \\times 2 + 0 \\times 1.5 = 2$.", why: "The blend $a_1 x_0 + b_1 x_1$ with all the weight on the clean anchor." }
        ],
        answer: "$a_1 = 1$, $b_1 = 0$, $\\tilde\\beta_1 = 0$, $\\tilde\\mu_1 = 2$: a zero-spread spike at exactly $x_0$. Given the clean point, the question \"what was the value before the first dose?\" answers itself, and the observed $x_1 = 1.5$ is rightly ignored &mdash; the general formula handles its own edge case with no patching." },

      { q: "Why can't we just use $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$? Reconstruct the full argument that the $x_0$-free reverse conditional is not directly computable, using Bayes' rule and the two-point dataset ($x_0 = \\pm 2$, equal odds) as your concrete illustration.",
        steps: [
          { do: "Write Bayes' rule for the flip: $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t) = \\dfrac{q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})\\, q(x_{t-1})}{q(x_t)}$.", why: "Part 2's rule, applied with no extra conditioning &mdash; this is the only exact route from the known forward direction to the wanted reverse one." },
          { do: "Check the first factor: $q(x_t \\mid x_{t-1})$ is the forward kernel &mdash; fully known.", why: "Part 6 fixed it by design; no learning involved." },
          { do: "Expand the marginal: $q(x_{t-1}) = \\int q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_0)\\, q(x_0)\\, dx_0$ &mdash; an average of known closed-form bells, weighted by the data distribution $q(x_0)$ (and $q(x_t)$ has the same structure).", why: "Total probability: a chain value can arise from any clean starting point, so you must average over all of them with their real-world frequencies." },
          { do: "Identify the blocker: $q(x_0)$ is the data cloud's rule &mdash; the very object the course is trying to learn. The flip is circular: the exact denoiser presupposes the answer.", why: "Parts 1-2 defined generation as sampling from exactly this unknown rule; if we had it, we would not need diffusion at all." },
          { do: "Make it concrete: with data at $\\pm 2$, an observed $x_2$ near 0 could have drifted from either side, so $q(x_1 \\mid x_2)$ has two bumps whose relative sizes depend on how often the data uses each side.", why: "A distribution over origins directly shapes the reverse conditional &mdash; change the data's balance to 90/10 and the bumps' sizes change with it, with no change to the noising recipe." }
        ],
        answer: "$q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t)$ requires the marginals $q(x_{t-1})$ and $q(x_t)$, which are data-distribution averages &mdash; unknowable without the very thing we want to learn, and generally non-Gaussian (two bumps in the toy). Conditioning on $x_0$ replaces both marginals with the fixed recipe's closed forms, which is exactly why the posterior of this part is computable." },

      { q: "Oracle-sampler reasoning. Explain (a) why the oracle sampler's final output always equals the oracle's $x_0$ exactly, and (b) why the run is still deeply informative &mdash; what do the intermediate steps demonstrate, and what single replacement turns the oracle sampler into a real generative model?",
        steps: [
          { do: "Compute the last step's parameters: at $t = 1$, $\\tilde\\beta_1 = 0$ and $\\tilde\\mu_1 = x_0$ (practice problem 5).", why: "Direct plug-in of the convention $\\bar\\alpha_0 = 1$ into the general formulas." },
          { do: "Conclude (a): the final draw is from a Gaussian with zero spread centered at $x_0$, i.e. it IS $x_0$, whatever noisy path the walk took to get there.", why: "A zero-variance Gaussian is a spike: all its probability sits on its center." },
          { do: "For (b), name what the intermediates prove: every step $t = T, \\dots, 2$ is a draw from the exact posterior $q(x_{t-1} \\mid x_t, x_0)$, so the run demonstrates that plain Gaussian arithmetic &mdash; blend two anchors, add $\\sqrt{\\tilde\\beta_t}$-sized noise &mdash; carries a pure-noise point all the way to data.", why: "The machinery of reverse-time generation is thereby verified end to end, with zero approximation, before any neural network exists." },
          { do: "Name the single replacement: swap the oracle's $x_0$ for a guess $\\hat{x}_0(x_t, t)$ produced by a trained network, and leave every other line of the loop unchanged.", why: "$x_0$ enters the step only through the blend $a_t x_0 + b_t x_t$, so any plug-in guess yields a runnable step; parts 8-9 build and train exactly this plug-in (via the equivalent noise-guess $\\epsilon_\\theta$)." }
        ],
        answer: "(a) Because $\\tilde\\beta_1 = 0$ and $\\tilde\\mu_1 = x_0$, the walk lands on the oracle's point exactly. (b) The intermediates prove the reverse-Gaussian machinery works &mdash; noise reassembles into data &mdash; isolating the one missing capability: guessing $x_0$ (equivalently the noise) from $x_t$, which is precisely what the network of parts 8-10 supplies." }
    ]
  });
})();
